{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Solar-absorber",
  "Notes": "Solar-selective absorber on steel: high solar absorptance, low thermal emittance\n\nCaveats:\n- Manufacturability is scored with MINT/MAXT (minimum and maximum layer thickness) at a low weight. A sensitivity term (SENS) would score robustness to thickness errors directly, but it needs second derivatives of the merit function and is not offered; MINT/MAXT keep layers depositable and have exact derivatives.\n- The specification is ASOL at least 0.93 over 300-2500 nm against AM1.5 global (ASTM G173-03), and EMIT at most 0.10 over 2.5-25 um against a blackbody at 373.15 K (100 C). Each row is one average over its band, integrated against the spectrum between its points. AERATIO, their ratio over 300 nm to 25 um, is reported and not scored.\n- The cermet and the steel are constant-index, so the cermet absorbs as strongly at 10 um as at 500 nm. Refine then trades ASOL against EMIT (about 0.86 against 0.19) and cannot meet both; a real cermet is transparent in the infrared, which needs dispersive optical constants.\n- The thermal band runs to 25 um, beyond where most optical constant data stops; check that the materials you substitute cover it.",
